[38;5;231m⏺[39m Here's what's actually in the repo:

  - [38;5;153mindex.html[39m — 11 lines: a [38;5;153m<h1>Tasks</h1>[39m and an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. That's the whole app.
  - One commit: "initial: empty tasks page."

  So there are no tasks, no users, no storage, and no server yet. "Notify users when tasks they care about change"
  presupposes all four. Before I design anything, I need to know what I'm building on.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether notifications are the first real feature of a system I also design (tasks, identity, persistence),
  or a layer over something that already exists somewhere I haven't seen. These lead to completely different designs —
  the first makes storage and identity part of this spec, the second makes them constraints I have to fit. Getting this
  wrong wastes the whole design, and it's the one thing I can't infer from the repo.

  - [1mGreenfield here[0m — I design the task model, a notion of "user", storage, and change detection as part of this.
    Broadest scope; also the only option where I get to pick clean boundaries. Nothing to redo later, but it's a
    multi-spec project, not one feature.
  - [1mReal app elsewhere[0m — this repo is a stand-in and the actual tasks/users/API live somewhere else. Cheapest correct
    design, but I need you to tell me the stack and data model; everything I'd guess would be wrong.
  - [1mSingle-user local demo[0m — no accounts, no server. "Notification" means an in-page badge/toast when a task changes in
    this browser. Smallest by far, throws away almost nothing since the UI layer survives if you add a backend later.

  My recommendation is the third [3mif[0m this is a prototype, because it's the only version that is one feature rather than
  one product — but I suspect the answer is the second and you have a real app in mind.
